{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The agent inside the sandbox always has its own "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"native tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Bash, file\nedits, and search, running directly on the sandbox filesystem. That covers\neverything the agent does locally to the working tree."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What it cannot do on its own is reach back into "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"your"}]},{"type":"text","value":" app: your database, your\nsecrets, the closures you captured when you defined a tool. For that, the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"-provided "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"bridged"}]},{"type":"text","value":" into the sandbox."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This page is about your own host tools, bridged back to the orchestrator. If\ninstead you want to give the agent third-party MCP servers it talks to\ndirectly with no host round-trip. Those are declared on the workspace, see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./provisioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provisioning"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For server tools in general, see the main\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/server-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" doc."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"native-vs-bridged-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native vs bridged tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" with a sandbox in the middleware, each tool is\nexposed to the in-sandbox agent over a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"host-side MCP tool-proxy"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The agent calls the tool by name, as it would any MCP tool."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The call is proxied back across the sandbox boundary to the host."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The tool's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"on the host"}]},{"type":"text","value":", keeping its DB handle, secrets,\nand any closures it captured."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The result is returned into the sandbox as the tool-call output."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" never ships into the sandbox; only the call and its result cross\nthe boundary. The tool keeps running where it was defined."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { repoSandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\nimport { getTodos } from './tools'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nchat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  // `execute()` closes over `db` and runs on the host, never in the sandbox.\n  tools: [\n    getTodos.server(async ({ userId }: { userId: string }) =>\n      db.todos.find({ userId }),\n    ),\n  ],\n  middleware: [withSandbox(repoSandbox)],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bridge is gated by a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"random per-run bearer token"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so the proxy endpoint\nis not an open door: only the agent for this run, holding that token, can\ninvoke your tools."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reaching-the-bridge","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaching the bridge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bridge is an HTTP endpoint the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sandbox calls back to"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For bridged tools\nto work, the sandbox has to be able to open a connection to your orchestrator.\nThat holds in two cases and breaks in a third."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Topology"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host the sandbox dials"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setup"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local process"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None. Works by default."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker container ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dockerSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None. Works by default."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker Sandboxes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guest URL stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":" proxy rewrites that host to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" before the policy match. A deny or ask allowlist must include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" when it writes a real allowlist."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployed orchestrator (production)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your public host, derived from the request"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None. Works by default."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your laptop, which has no public URL"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tunnel the bridge with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withNgrokBridge"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"local-process-docker-container","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local process / Docker container"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The orchestrator is the same machine as the sandbox, reached on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" (local-process) or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"},{"type":"text","value":" (Docker container). Bridged tools work with no extra configuration. If the HTTP listener cannot start, bridge startup rejects with the original Node.js error."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"docker-sandboxes-sbxsandbox","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker Sandboxes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guest still dials "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"},{"type":"text","value":". The host HTTP proxy rewrites\nthat host to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" before it checks "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx policy"},{"type":"text","value":". If you write a deny\nor ask allowlist, allow "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" (or let "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" add it).\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":" alone does not write that allowlist."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"a-deployed-orchestrator-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A deployed orchestrator (production)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A deployed orchestrator already has a public URL, so the bridge is reachable out\nof the box. The provisioner advertises your public host (derived from the\nincoming request, instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":", and every call is still gated by the\nper-run bearer token, so exposing the endpoint publicly is safe. This is the\nsame path the edge/Cloudflare deployment uses; see "},{"type":"link","href":"./cloudflare","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"a-remote-cloud-sandbox-driven-from-your-laptop","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With a cloud provider (Daytona, Vercel, Sprites) in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"local dev"}]},{"type":"text","value":", the sandbox is a remote\nVM. It "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cannot dial your machine's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and your laptop has no public\nURL, so bridged tools can't reach the host until you expose the bridge."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox/ngrok"},{"type":"text","value":" subpath tunnels the loopback bridge through\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://ngrok.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ngrok"}]},{"type":"text","value":" so a remote sandbox can reach it. Set\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NGROK_AUTHTOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":", then add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withNgrokBridge"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"after"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox(...)"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { withNgrokBridge } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/ngrok'\nimport { repoSandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\nimport { getTodos } from './tools'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nchat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  tools: [\n    getTodos.server(async ({ userId }: { userId: string }) =>\n      db.todos.find({ userId }),\n    ),\n  ],\n  // Cloud provider in local dev → tunnel the host bridge so the remote sandbox\n  // can reach it. Local process / Docker don't need this.\n  middleware: [withSandbox(repoSandbox), withNgrokBridge],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ngrok/ngrok"},{"type":"text","value":" is an "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"optional peer dependency"}]},{"type":"text","value":", install it alongside the\nsubpath ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"npm i @ngrok/ngrok"},{"type":"text","value":"). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withNgrokBridge"},{"type":"text","value":" is purely a local-dev\nconvenience: in production your deployed orchestrator is already reachable, so\nyou ship without it."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"native-vs-bridged-tools","text":"Native vs bridged tools","level":2},{"id":"reaching-the-bridge","text":"Reaching the bridge","level":2},{"id":"local-process-docker-container","text":"Local process / Docker container","level":3},{"id":"docker-sandboxes-sbxsandbox","text":"Docker Sandboxes (sbxSandbox)","level":3},{"id":"a-deployed-orchestrator-production","text":"A deployed orchestrator (production)","level":3},{"id":"a-remote-cloud-sandbox-driven-from-your-laptop","text":"A remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe agent inside the sandbox always has its own **native tools**: Bash, file\nedits, and search, running directly on the sandbox filesystem. That covers\neverything the agent does locally to the working tree.\n\nWhat it cannot do on its own is reach back into _your_ app: your database, your\nsecrets, the closures you captured when you defined a tool. For that, the\n`chat()`-provided **server tools** are **bridged** into the sandbox.\n\n> This page is about your own host tools, bridged back to the orchestrator. If\n> instead you want to give the agent third-party MCP servers it talks to\n> directly with no host round-trip. Those are declared on the workspace, see\n> [Provisioning](./provisioning). For server tools in general, see the main\n> [server tools](../tools/server-tools) doc.\n\n## Native vs bridged tools\n\nWhen you pass `tools` to `chat()` with a sandbox in the middleware, each tool is\nexposed to the in-sandbox agent over a **host-side MCP tool-proxy**:\n\n1. The agent calls the tool by name, as it would any MCP tool.\n2. The call is proxied back across the sandbox boundary to the host.\n3. The tool's `execute()` runs **on the host**, keeping its DB handle, secrets,\n   and any closures it captured.\n4. The result is returned into the sandbox as the tool-call output.\n\nSo `execute()` never ships into the sandbox; only the call and its result cross\nthe boundary. The tool keeps running where it was defined.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { repoSandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\nimport { getTodos } from './tools'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nchat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  // `execute()` closes over `db` and runs on the host, never in the sandbox.\n  tools: [\n    getTodos.server(async ({ userId }: { userId: string }) =>\n      db.todos.find({ userId }),\n    ),\n  ],\n  middleware: [withSandbox(repoSandbox)],\n})\n```\n\nThe bridge is gated by a **random per-run bearer token**, so the proxy endpoint\nis not an open door: only the agent for this run, holding that token, can\ninvoke your tools.\n\n## Reaching the bridge\n\nThe bridge is an HTTP endpoint the **sandbox calls back to**. For bridged tools\nto work, the sandbox has to be able to open a connection to your orchestrator.\nThat holds in two cases and breaks in a third.\n\n| Topology | Host the sandbox dials | Setup |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local process | `localhost` | None. Works by default. |\n| Docker container (`dockerSandbox`) | `host.docker.internal` | None. Works by default. |\n| Docker Sandboxes (`sbxSandbox`) | `host.docker.internal` | The guest URL stays `host.docker.internal`. The `sbx` proxy rewrites that host to `localhost` before the policy match. A deny or ask allowlist must include `localhost`. `sbxSandbox()` adds `localhost` when it writes a real allowlist. |\n| Deployed orchestrator (production) | Your public host, derived from the request | None. Works by default. |\n| Remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop | Your laptop, which has no public URL | Tunnel the bridge with `withNgrokBridge`. |\n\n### Local process / Docker container\n\nThe orchestrator is the same machine as the sandbox, reached on `localhost` (local-process) or `host.docker.internal` (Docker container). Bridged tools work with no extra configuration. If the HTTP listener cannot start, bridge startup rejects with the original Node.js error.\n\n### Docker Sandboxes (`sbxSandbox`)\n\nThe guest still dials `host.docker.internal`. The host HTTP proxy rewrites\nthat host to `localhost` before it checks `sbx policy`. If you write a deny\nor ask allowlist, allow `localhost` (or let `sbxSandbox()` add it).\n`denyNetwork` alone does not write that allowlist.\n\n### A deployed orchestrator (production)\n\nA deployed orchestrator already has a public URL, so the bridge is reachable out\nof the box. The provisioner advertises your public host (derived from the\nincoming request, instead of `localhost`, and every call is still gated by the\nper-run bearer token, so exposing the endpoint publicly is safe. This is the\nsame path the edge/Cloudflare deployment uses; see [Cloudflare](./cloudflare).\n\n### A remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop\n\nWith a cloud provider (Daytona, Vercel, Sprites) in **local dev**, the sandbox is a remote\nVM. It **cannot dial your machine's `localhost`**, and your laptop has no public\nURL, so bridged tools can't reach the host until you expose the bridge.\n\nThe `@tanstack/ai-sandbox/ngrok` subpath tunnels the loopback bridge through\n[ngrok](https://ngrok.com) so a remote sandbox can reach it. Set\n`NGROK_AUTHTOKEN`, then add `withNgrokBridge` **after** `withSandbox(...)`:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { withNgrokBridge } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/ngrok'\nimport { repoSandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\nimport { getTodos } from './tools'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nchat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  tools: [\n    getTodos.server(async ({ userId }: { userId: string }) =>\n      db.todos.find({ userId }),\n    ),\n  ],\n  // Cloud provider in local dev → tunnel the host bridge so the remote sandbox\n  // can reach it. Local process / Docker don't need this.\n  middleware: [withSandbox(repoSandbox), withNgrokBridge],\n})\n```\n\n`@ngrok/ngrok` is an **optional peer dependency**, install it alongside the\nsubpath (`npm i @ngrok/ngrok`). `withNgrokBridge` is purely a local-dev\nconvenience: in production your deployed orchestrator is already reachable, so\nyou ship without it.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Tools\nid: tools\norder: 6\ndescription: \"Bridge your app's host tools (with their DB, secrets, and closures) into the in-sandbox agent over an authenticated MCP tool-proxy.\"\n---\n\nThe agent inside the sandbox always has its own **native tools**: Bash, file\nedits, and search, running directly on the sandbox filesystem. That covers\neverything the agent does locally to the working tree.\n\nWhat it cannot do on its own is reach back into _your_ app: your database, your\nsecrets, the closures you captured when you defined a tool. For that, the\n`chat()`-provided **server tools** are **bridged** into the sandbox.\n\n> This page is about your own host tools, bridged back to the orchestrator. If\n> instead you want to give the agent third-party MCP servers it talks to\n> directly with no host round-trip. Those are declared on the workspace, see\n> [Provisioning](./provisioning). For server tools in general, see the main\n> [server tools](../tools/server-tools) doc.\n\n## Native vs bridged tools\n\nWhen you pass `tools` to `chat()` with a sandbox in the middleware, each tool is\nexposed to the in-sandbox agent over a **host-side MCP tool-proxy**:\n\n1. The agent calls the tool by name, as it would any MCP tool.\n2. The call is proxied back across the sandbox boundary to the host.\n3. The tool's `execute()` runs **on the host**, keeping its DB handle, secrets,\n   and any closures it captured.\n4. The result is returned into the sandbox as the tool-call output.\n\nSo `execute()` never ships into the sandbox; only the call and its result cross\nthe boundary. The tool keeps running where it was defined.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { repoSandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\nimport { getTodos } from './tools'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nchat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  // `execute()` closes over `db` and runs on the host, never in the sandbox.\n  tools: [\n    getTodos.server(async ({ userId }: { userId: string }) =>\n      db.todos.find({ userId }),\n    ),\n  ],\n  middleware: [withSandbox(repoSandbox)],\n})\n```\n\nThe bridge is gated by a **random per-run bearer token**, so the proxy endpoint\nis not an open door: only the agent for this run, holding that token, can\ninvoke your tools.\n\n## Reaching the bridge\n\nThe bridge is an HTTP endpoint the **sandbox calls back to**. For bridged tools\nto work, the sandbox has to be able to open a connection to your orchestrator.\nThat holds in two cases and breaks in a third.\n\n| Topology | Host the sandbox dials | Setup |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local process | `localhost` | None. Works by default. |\n| Docker container (`dockerSandbox`) | `host.docker.internal` | None. Works by default. |\n| Docker Sandboxes (`sbxSandbox`) | `host.docker.internal` | The guest URL stays `host.docker.internal`. The `sbx` proxy rewrites that host to `localhost` before the policy match. A deny or ask allowlist must include `localhost`. `sbxSandbox()` adds `localhost` when it writes a real allowlist. |\n| Deployed orchestrator (production) | Your public host, derived from the request | None. Works by default. |\n| Remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop | Your laptop, which has no public URL | Tunnel the bridge with `withNgrokBridge`. |\n\n### Local process / Docker container\n\nThe orchestrator is the same machine as the sandbox, reached on `localhost` (local-process) or `host.docker.internal` (Docker container). Bridged tools work with no extra configuration. If the HTTP listener cannot start, bridge startup rejects with the original Node.js error.\n\n### Docker Sandboxes (`sbxSandbox`)\n\nThe guest still dials `host.docker.internal`. The host HTTP proxy rewrites\nthat host to `localhost` before it checks `sbx policy`. If you write a deny\nor ask allowlist, allow `localhost` (or let `sbxSandbox()` add it).\n`denyNetwork` alone does not write that allowlist.\n\n### A deployed orchestrator (production)\n\nA deployed orchestrator already has a public URL, so the bridge is reachable out\nof the box. The provisioner advertises your public host (derived from the\nincoming request, instead of `localhost`, and every call is still gated by the\nper-run bearer token, so exposing the endpoint publicly is safe. This is the\nsame path the edge/Cloudflare deployment uses; see [Cloudflare](./cloudflare).\n\n### A remote cloud sandbox, driven from your laptop\n\nWith a cloud provider (Daytona, Vercel, Sprites) in **local dev**, the sandbox is a remote\nVM. It **cannot dial your machine's `localhost`**, and your laptop has no public\nURL, so bridged tools can't reach the host until you expose the bridge.\n\nThe `@tanstack/ai-sandbox/ngrok` subpath tunnels the loopback bridge through\n[ngrok](https://ngrok.com) so a remote sandbox can reach it. Set\n`NGROK_AUTHTOKEN`, then add `withNgrokBridge` **after** `withSandbox(...)`:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { withNgrokBridge } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/ngrok'\nimport { repoSandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\nimport { getTodos } from './tools'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nchat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  tools: [\n    getTodos.server(async ({ userId }: { userId: string }) =>\n      db.todos.find({ userId }),\n    ),\n  ],\n  // Cloud provider in local dev → tunnel the host bridge so the remote sandbox\n  // can reach it. Local process / Docker don't need this.\n  middleware: [withSandbox(repoSandbox), withNgrokBridge],\n})\n```\n\n`@ngrok/ngrok` is an **optional peer dependency**, install it alongside the\nsubpath (`npm i @ngrok/ngrok`). `withNgrokBridge` is purely a local-dev\nconvenience: in production your deployed orchestrator is already reachable, so\nyou ship without it.\n"}
